{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs. realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply a downward level shift (larger for longer horizons).",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: underperformance increases with lead time (errors small in first week, then widen materially). Use a horizon-based discount (e.g., subtract more €/t as horizon increases).",
    "Overestimation of rebounds/mean reversion: model repeatedly predicts stronger recoveries after declines than actually occur. Damp mean-reversion strength (reduce forecast slope back up after drawdowns).",
    "Downtrend lag: in sustained sell-offs the model follows the direction but not the magnitude (too slow/too shallow). Increase responsiveness to recent negative momentum (steepen projected declines when last 1–2 weeks are falling).",
    "Overshoot on local peaks: when the model projects short-term upticks, it tends to overshoot the subsequent realized highs. Cap near-term bounce amplitudes and smooth local maxima."
  ]
}
